foreign  welcome to another installment of Harry  Potter Theory today we're going to be  discussing the darker side of the  Wizarding World diving deep into the  subject of horcruxes And discussing a  historical figure who created a horcrux  long before Voldemort ever did JK  Rowling once revealed that over the  course of history many witches and  wizards tried to create a horcrux in  spite of a severe lack of instructions  from Evil textbooks Wizards would have  been looking for ways to do exactly what  Voldemort did for years and some of the  ways they would have tried would have  killed them so I imagine it well they're  huge parallels splitting the atom would  be a very good parallel in our world I  do imagine that other people are going  to have tried I think it would be naive  not to think that people have been  trying for a long time and thought they  succeeded and hadn't or else maim  themselves or killed themselves in the  attempt it's such a dangerous thing to  do  what this quote tells me is that maybe  just maybe there's a wizard out there  that was able to produce a horcrux  before anyone else they just never made  it into the history books after all if  the idea of producing a horcrux is  self-preservation why would you want to  boast about it and bring it to  everyone's attention  I happen to have one very good guess for  who this wizard might be the original  horcrux Creator but first let's look at  the other Wizards in Harry Potter Canon  who are credited as having created  horcruxes and let's look at what exactly  a horcrux is a horcrux is a dark object  in which a dark Witcher wizard has  imbued part of their soul the ultimate  purse of creating a horcrux is to  achieve immortality JK Rowling who  coined the term defines it as follows a  receptacle prepared by dark magic in  which a dark wizard has intentionally  hidden a fragment of his soul for the  purpose of attaining immortality  according to Harry Potter Canon herpo  the fowl is the first wizard on record  to have created a horcrux  herpo was an ancient dark wizard that  hailed from Greece who happened to have  an awful lot of similarities to Salazar  sutherin he was a parcel mouth and he  created the first ever basilisk which  could perhaps suggest that Slytherin was  a descendant of her pose it's entirely  unknown when herpa walked the Earth but  we do know that it was a long long time  ago as he is commonly referred to as one  of the earliest known dark Wizards while  herpo is credited as having created the  first horcrux what's surprising is that  we actually don't know much about it we  don't know when he made it what it is or  if when it was destroyed  for all we know herpo could still be  around I've Got a Theory surrounding  this in another video The Lost horcrux  but go and watch that afterwards the  next credited horcrux Creator came along  many many centuries later a young boy by  the name of Tom Riddle Voldemort I would  venture to say that Voldemort is the  individual most commonly associated with  horcruxes not herpo despite herpo being  the first to create one and that's  because Voldemort whose main objective  in life was to achieve immortality  ultimately created seven of them a ring  diarrhea cup a locket a diadem a snake  and a boy so if not herpo and not  Voldemort who is this mysterious wizard  that I believe should be credited as the  first horcrux Creator if you're not up  to Snuff on your folklore particularly  your Russian folklore then the concept  of a soul container or Soul Shard like a  horcrux may have been introduced to you  in the Harry Potter books but as it so  happens this is not a novel concept and  it was actually introduced many many  centuries ago in folk tales now it's no  secret that JK Rowling tends to draw all  kinds of inspiration from all over the  map when doing her storytelling there's  Greek influence Roman influence historic  literary influence and well many other  kinds of influence evident within the  Harry Potter story and Rowling certainly  gives due credit to these sources of  inspiration however when questioned  specifically about the origin of  horcruxes JK Rowling claimed that  nothing in particular had inspired the  concept could this be the case certainly  but does this piece of Russian folklore  that I'm about to tell you seem quite  familiar you be the judge and let me  know in the comments the first folktale  containing a horcrux-esque object was  called zarovich Peter and The Wizard in  this story The tsar's Three Sons attempt  to rescue Tsar bellinin's wife these  aritza who has been abducted by a dark  wizard kosher  kosher who also goes by the epithet The  Immortal which Bears a striking  resemblance to Voldemort Voldemort  flight of death in French is an  archetypal male antagonist that has been  appearing in Slavic Tales since before  the 18th century in this tale two of the  tsar's sons fell in their attempts to  rescue these Ariza but the third son  Peter discovers the secret to kosher's  immortality koche much like Voldemort  discovered a dark spell that allowed him  to separate soul from body hiding his  soul inside of nested objects to  protected  the tale of crochet supports the idea in  Harry Potter that the soul is the most  important part of our life force and  that the physical body is merely a  container a vessel in order to prevent  himself from being killed nkoshay hides  his soul inside of nested objects in  order to protect it like the Russian  Matryoshka doll or nesting doll there  are layers and layers that need to be  peeled back in order to get to the base  layer each layer acting as one more  measure of protection  in kosher's tale his soul is hidden  within an egg within a duck within a  hair that nests in a hollow log that  floats on a pond found in a forest on  the island of bouyan in some versions  kosher's soul is hidden within a needle  within an egg within a duck with the  duck flying away whenever anyone tries  to catch It ultimately in the story of  Peter and kosher Peter is able to find  the egg and destroy him  some have speculated that crochet was  inspired by 12th century balotzsian  leader named Khan konchak kosher's  epithet The Immortal could potentially  be a reference to contract's longevity  as contract was able to live an  unusually long life despite it being the  12th century contract purportedly lived  for over 100 years which at the time  would have been the equivalent of many  generations though kosher's method of  Soul encasement differs from Harry  Potter in some ways it's hard to ignore  the parallels between crochet and  Voldemort and I for one thing that  kosher at least subconsciously if not  consciously inspired rolling's concept  of the horcrux both men created a  multitude of horcruxes by hiding  encasing Soul fragments into objects and  both of these men's objects happen to be  very difficult to find and provided that  kosher existed long before her put a  foul or Voldemort it's entirely possible  that he could be credited as the first  horcrux Creator even in the Harry Potter  Universe for all we know koche could be  related to the founders of the Russian  magical school called of stovoriettes  called  was apparently founded around the time  of the Novgorod Republic which checks  out time wise what do you guys think let  me know in the comments section below  until next time remember greatness  inspires Envy Envy engenders spite spite  spawns lies  [Music]  [Music]

